window.HDH_SEARCH=[{"t":"Getting started","s":"","u":"docs/getting-started/","x":"SheetPilot turns a spreadsheet into an interactive dashboard in your browser. There is nothing to install, no account to create, and your file is never uploaded."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Open your first sheet","u":"docs/getting-started/#open-your-first-sheet","x":"1. Open SheetPilot and click Import sheet . 2. Choose a file, or paste cells copied from Excel or Google Sheets. No file to hand? Click one of the samples: Tickets , Sales , Reviews , Customers or Salaries . For the best results, upload the Excel (.xlsx) file rather than a CSV. An Excel file keeps your real formulas, every tab and proper dates, so SheetPilot can show how the numbers are calculated and check them. A CSV has only the final values, so formulas have to be guessed. From Google Sheets: File → Download → Microsoft Excel (.xlsx) . 3. Click Import . !The import dialog You land on the D"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"What you get","u":"docs/getting-started/#what-you-get","x":"- Key numbers and findings in plain language. - A comparison chart (for example, average resolution time by priority). Click a bar to filter everything. - A trend over time, a distribution , and a list of the rows that need attention. - A heatmap when the sheet has scored criteria. - A How the sheet works section with the formulas and data checks. !A dashboard built from a sample support-ticket sheet"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"The four tabs","u":"docs/getting-started/#the-four-tabs","x":"Tab What it is for Dashboard The overview. Filter, compare, restyle (palette icon), export and share. Reminders Dates that need attention, if your sheet has follow-up or review dates. See Reminders and the table. Table Your rows, editable, with search and sorting. Setup Change how the sheet is read, and the type of each column."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Your sheets are saved in your browser","u":"docs/getting-started/#your-sheets-are-saved-in-your-browser","x":"Imported sheets are kept in your browser's storage on this computer, so they are still there next time. They are not synced between devices. Use Export to keep a copy (see Export and share), and note that clearing your browser's site data removes them. Next: How SheetPilot reads your sheet."},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/","x":"Nothing needs to be set up. SheetPilot looks at the column names, the values and the relationships between columns, and makes a series of choices. It always tells you what it chose, and you can change any of it."},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"The \"How I read your sheet\" box","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/#the-how-i-read-your-sheet-box","x":"After an import, the top of the dashboard shows what SheetPilot decided: !The review box - Each row is named by a column such as Name or Ticket ID , used in lists. - Main number : the measure the dashboard is built around, shown as a total or an average . - Which direction is good : hours, cost and churn are lower is better ; scores, revenue and ratings are higher is better ; anything else has no good or bad direction. - Compared by : the column that best explains differences in the main number. - Timeline : the date column that works as a timeline ( Created rather than Hire date ). A yellow u"},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"How the choices are made","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/#how-the-choices-are-made","x":"Choice How it is decided Main number Numeric columns are scored by their name (score, revenue, salary, hours…), their type (percent, money) and how many rows are filled in. ID-like numbers are ignored. Total or average Revenue, budget, cost and quantity columns are added up. Salary, price, time and score columns are averaged. Direction Names such as time , hours , cost , churn or error mean lower is better. Score , revenue , rating or satisfaction mean higher is better. Compared by For every candidate column, SheetPilot measures how much of the variation in the main number is explained by the "},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"Changing the choices","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/#changing-the-choices","x":"There are three ways, and your changes are remembered for that sheet: - The dropdowns above the comparison chart: average or total , measure , compared by and direction . - Setup → How SheetPilot reads this sheet , with an Auto option for each choice and Reset to automatic . - Setup → Column types , if a column was read as the wrong type (for example text instead of a date)."},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"Formulas and logic","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/#formulas-and-logic","x":"SheetPilot also works out how columns are calculated from each other. - With an Excel file , it shows your real formulas in plain words, using column names instead of cell references: =SUM([Quality of work]:[Customer focus]) . - With a CSV (which keeps values but not formulas), it infers rules from the numbers: sums, products, ratios and copies. For example Revenue = Units × Unit price , with a note of whether it holds in every row. For the best results, upload the Excel (.xlsx) file. Inference can only guess; the real formulas are exact, and they also explain special cases such as \"N/A counts"},{"t":"How SheetPilot reads your sheet","s":"Findings and data checks","u":"docs/how-it-reads-your-sheet/#findings-and-data-checks","x":"The Key findings box writes a few sentences about what stands out: the biggest group, the best and worst group, how much a column explains, trends, and extreme outliers. When nothing stands out, it says so. The data checks list empty columns, entries such as \"N/A\" in number columns, and repeated IDs. Next: The dashboard."},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"","u":"docs/the-dashboard/","x":"The dashboard is built from what SheetPilot understood about your sheet. Every section responds to the filters. !The dashboard"},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"Filter and compare","u":"docs/the-dashboard/#filter-and-compare","x":"- Use the dropdowns under the style picker to filter by any group column, such as priority or agent. - Click a bar in the comparison chart, or a name in the heatmap or findings, to filter the whole page. Click it again to clear it. - Above the chart, change average / total / highest / lowest , the measure , the grouping and which direction is good . The best group turns green and the worst red, when a direction applies. !The comparison chart"},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"The sections","u":"docs/the-dashboard/#the-sections","x":"Section What it shows Key numbers Row count and the main numbers. Yes/no columns show how many are true. Key findings Plain-language observations. See How SheetPilot reads your sheet. Comparison chart The main number by group. Click a bar to filter. Trend over time Grouped by day, month or year depending on the span. Criteria heatmap Scored columns by group, green for high and red for low. Distribution How the main number is spread. Highest or lowest rows The rows that need attention, based on the direction that is good. How the sheet works The understood structure, formulas and data checks. !"},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"Style it: template, font and colour","u":"docs/the-dashboard/#style-it-template-font-and-colour","x":"Click the palette icon in the top bar. The panel has three parts, and every change applies straight away: !The style panel - Template : fourteen looks that differ in more than colour. They change the typography, the outlines (solid, dotted, dashed or double), shadows, spacing and the shape of the charts. The professional ones come first and have no background patterns. - Font : keep the template's own, or switch to Sans, Serif, Mono, Rounded, Humanist or Slab. - Accent colour : keep the template's, or pick one of eight colours."},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"Professional templates","u":"docs/the-dashboard/#professional-templates","x":"Template Look Suits Executive Clean white cards, soft shadows, green accent. The default Any team Graphite Neutral greys and fine lines Formal reports; prints well Slate A calm dark mode, with no glow or gradients Screens, dim rooms, dashboards on a wall Analyst Compact, navy, uppercase labels Finance, operations, BI Sheet Green and white with fine grid lines, like a spreadsheet Spreadsheet fans Boardroom Navy, serif headings, accent rules Finance, consulting Minimal Hairlines, light uppercase type, lots of white Design, architecture, luxury !The Graphite template !The Slate template !The Anal"},{"t":"The dashboard","s":"Expressive templates","u":"docs/the-dashboard/#expressive-templates","x":"Template Look Suits Editorial Serif type, double rules, small caps Media, research, legal Midnight Dark with a soft glow and gradient bars Tech, SaaS, product Soft Frosted cards, big radii, pastel gradients Wellness, beauty, healthcare Blueprint Dashed outlines, mono type, graph paper Engineering, construction, IT Notebook Dotted outlines, ruled paper, handwritten headings Education, small teams Playful Thick outlines, hard shadows, big bars Retail, startups, marketing Terminal Green on black, mono, dashed Developers, security, ops !The Blueprint template !The Notebook template The choice is s"},{"t":"Export and share","s":"","u":"docs/export-and-share/","x":"Both start from the dashboard you are looking at, including your current filters and style."},{"t":"Export and share","s":"Export","u":"docs/export-and-share/#export","x":"Click Export… , tick the sections to include, and choose a format: !The export dialog Format What you get Web page (.html) One self-contained file in your chosen style. It opens in any browser, without internet, and keeps its charts and findings. Controls are removed. PDF Opens the print dialog in a new tab. Choose Save as PDF . Data (.csv) The rows currently shown, without charts. The section ticks do not apply. If you applied a filter, the export says so at the top, so nobody mistakes a partial view for the whole sheet. Every export carries a credits footer."},{"t":"Export and share","s":"Share","u":"docs/export-and-share/#share","x":"Click Share… , choose what to include, and create a link: !The share dialog - View-only snapshot : contains only the ticked sections, as they look now. No raw rows are included. Opening it shows the dashboard without any controls. - Interactive copy : contains every row, so the recipient can filter, compare and change the style. They can save a copy to their own sheets. Their changes are never sent back to you. !A shared snapshot"},{"t":"Export and share","s":"How sharing works","u":"docs/export-and-share/#how-sharing-works","x":"There is no server. The link itself holds the data, compressed, after the in the address, and that part of a web address is never sent to the web server. This keeps SheetPilot free and private, and it means: - Anyone who has the link can read what is in it. Do not share confidential sheets this way, and note that chat apps and email systems may keep a copy of the link. - Links cannot be revoked or updated. Create a new one if something changes. - Long links (over about 30 KB) can be cut off by some chat apps. Large sheets may be too big for a link, in which case SheetPilot suggests exporting a"},{"t":"Reminders and the table","s":"","u":"docs/reminders-and-table/","x":"Some sheets are not only for reporting: they also track things that need doing. SheetPilot can turn date columns into reminders."},{"t":"Reminders and the table","s":"Reminders","u":"docs/reminders-and-table/#reminders","x":"Open Setup → Dates that trigger reminders and tick the date columns that matter, such as Next follow-up or Review date . Use the offset to shift the reminder: 3 is three days after the date, -30 is thirty days before it (useful for contract end dates). !The Reminders tab The Reminders tab then lists what is overdue, due today and coming up in the next 14 days. For each item you can: - +7d to move the date a week later, or - Done to clear the reminder (it comes back only if the date changes). If your sheet has a status column , rows with a finished status (won, lost, closed, done, resigned…) ar"},{"t":"Reminders and the table","s":"The table","u":"docs/reminders-and-table/#the-table","x":"!The Table tab - Click a column heading to sort , and use the box to search . - Click a cell to edit it. Yes/no cells toggle with a click. - Click ↗ at the start of a row to open it. There you can read long text and add notes , which count as activity. - + Row adds a row. Edits are saved in your browser, and the dashboard updates with them. !Setup Next: Messy sheets and supported files."},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"","u":"docs/messy-sheets/","x":"Real spreadsheets are rarely tidy. SheetPilot is built to cope with the common problems."},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"Files","u":"docs/messy-sheets/#files","x":"You have What happens An Excel file (.xlsx, .xls) — recommended Every tab is read, dates stay dates, and your real formulas are shown in plain words. Tabs that only contain formulas and no data (empty monthly templates) are skipped, with a note. A CSV file, or tab or semicolon separated text Read directly, and the separator is detected for you. A CSV has only values, so formulas are inferred from the numbers instead. Cells copied from Excel or Google Sheets Paste them into the import dialog. Google Sheets Download as Excel (.xlsx) for the best results (File → Download), or copy the cells. Ther"},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"Headers and labels","u":"docs/messy-sheets/#headers-and-labels","x":"- A title row or label rows above the headers are skipped: SheetPilot finds the real header row. - Repeated or very long headers , such as the same \"0 = not met, 3 = partly, 5 = OK\" over several columns, are replaced with the short name from the row above, and the long text becomes a tooltip. - Cells to the right of the table , such as a legend, are ignored."},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"Dates","u":"docs/messy-sheets/#dates","x":"- Dates written 2026-10-08 , 08.10.2026 or 10/8/2026 are all understood. - If the order is unclear (day first or month first), SheetPilot decides from the rest of the column: a value such as 25.03.2026 shows that it is day first, dots usually mean day first, and a stray ambiguous value is resolved by its neighbouring dates. It tells you when it had to guess."},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"Side-by-side tables","u":"docs/messy-sheets/#side-by-side-tables","x":"Price lists and similar layouts, where several blocks sit next to each other under shared headings, are converted into one row per block, section, item and plan. A note on the sheet says when that happened. This works for blocks that have a header row and numbers beneath it."},{"t":"Messy sheets and supported files","s":"What it cannot do","u":"docs/messy-sheets/#what-it-cannot-do","x":"- It does not run macros, pivot tables or charts from the original file. - Conditional or lookup formulas are shown but not evaluated; the values in the file are used. - Very large files (hundreds of thousands of rows) become slow, because everything runs on your device. - Merged cells in the data area may need a clean-up first. If a column is read as the wrong type, change it in Setup → Column types . Next: Reminders and the table."},{"t":"Privacy and security","s":"","u":"docs/privacy-and-security/","x":""},{"t":"Privacy and security","s":"Your data stays on your computer","u":"docs/privacy-and-security/#your-data-stays-on-your-computer","x":"SheetPilot is a web page that runs entirely in your browser. When you import a file, it is read on your device. It is not uploaded , there is no account, and there is no server that receives your data. - Your sheets are stored in your browser's own storage on this computer only. - The app has no analytics, no cookies and no ads . - The project website, where you read this, counts visits anonymously with Cloudflare Web Analytics, with no cookies and no personal data. The app page does not include that counter. - The Excel reader is loaded from a public CDN the first time you import an Excel fil"},{"t":"Privacy and security","s":"Sharing and exporting","u":"docs/privacy-and-security/#sharing-and-exporting","x":"- An exported web page is a file you control. Treat it like the data it shows. - A share link contains the data it shares inside the link. Only share what you are happy for the recipient to read, and remember that messaging apps may store links. See Export and share. - Snapshots open in a sandbox with scripts disabled. Interactive copies are parsed as plain data and shown with all text escaped, so a link cannot run code."},{"t":"Privacy and security","s":"Sensitive sheets","u":"docs/privacy-and-security/#sensitive-sheets","x":"SheetPilot has no accounts or per-column permissions. It is a good fit for looking at a sensitive sheet (salaries, customers) privately on your own computer. It is not a way to control who sees it. If you share or export, you decide what leaves your computer."},{"t":"Privacy and security","s":"Open source","u":"docs/privacy-and-security/#open-source","x":"The code is public, so you can read how it works, run it from a local folder, or host it yourself. Report a problem on GitHub."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"","u":"docs/faq/","x":""},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does my file get uploaded anywhere?","u":"docs/faq/#does-my-file-get-uploaded-anywhere","x":"No. SheetPilot runs in your browser and reads your file on your device. See Privacy and security."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"What can I open, and what works best?","u":"docs/faq/#what-can-i-open-and-what-works-best","x":"Excel files (.xlsx and .xls, every tab), CSV or other delimited text, and cells pasted from Excel or Google Sheets. Excel is best: it keeps formulas, tabs and dates, which CSV loses. See Messy sheets and supported files."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Can it connect to Google Sheets and stay in sync?","u":"docs/faq/#can-it-connect-to-google-sheets-and-stay-in-sync","x":"Not yet. Download the sheet as CSV or Excel, or paste the cells. A live connection would need sign-in and a server, which SheetPilot deliberately does not have."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"It picked the wrong main number or grouping. What do I do?","u":"docs/faq/#it-picked-the-wrong-main-number-or-grouping-what-do-i-do","x":"Use the dropdowns above the comparison chart, or Setup → How SheetPilot reads this sheet . Your choice is remembered. See How SheetPilot reads your sheet."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Why does it say \"lower is better\" (or the opposite)?","u":"docs/faq/#why-does-it-say-lower-is-better-or-the-opposite","x":"The direction is guessed from the column name: hours, cost and churn are lower-is-better, scores and revenue higher-is-better. If it is wrong, change it next to the chart. Anything it cannot judge is shown with no good or bad colouring."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it work in other languages?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-work-in-other-languages","x":"Dates, numbers and text are handled in any language, but the name-based guesses (which column is the main number, which direction is good) are written for English column names. In other languages it plays safe, uses averages and shows no good or bad direction."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"My sheets disappeared.","u":"docs/faq/#my-sheets-disappeared","x":"Sheets are saved in your browser's storage. Clearing site data, using a private window, or switching browser or computer starts you fresh. Export a copy to keep one."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"How big a file can I use?","u":"docs/faq/#how-big-a-file-can-i-use","x":"Tens of thousands of rows are fine. Hundreds of thousands will be slow because everything runs on your device."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is it free?","u":"docs/faq/#is-it-free","x":"Yes, and open source. If it helps you, tell someone who lives in spreadsheets."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Who makes it?","u":"docs/faq/#who-makes-it","x":"George Stathopoulos. The CV, other projects and contact details are on that site."}];